{
 "manufacturer": "Studer Innotec",
 "manufacturer_slug": "studer-innotec",
 "model": "Xcom-CAN in BMS mode (lithium battery link, via Xcom-485i)",
 "model_slug": "xcom-can-bms",
 "short_model": "Xcom-CAN BMS",
 "aliases": [
  "Xcom-CAN",
  "Xcom-CAN BMS",
  "Xcom-485i"
 ],
 "equipment_type": "Battery inverter system (lithium battery BMS interface)",
 "applies_to": "Studer Xcom-CAN set to BMS mode (CAN link to a lithium battery BMS), read through an Xcom-485i Modbus RTU gateway, system release R706.",
 "summary": "In BMS mode the Studer Xcom-CAN links a lithium battery's BMS to the Xtender system and reports the battery's own values. An Xcom-485i on the Studer bus answers Modbus RTU for it. This map lists the Xcom-CAN BMS user infos (voltage, current, state of charge, BMS limits) as input registers and its parameters as holding registers.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": 61,
  "baud": null,
  "parity": "Even",
  "data_bits": 8,
  "stop_bits": 1,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "0-based",
  "note": "The appendix lists the register exactly as it goes in the request. The protocol spec's example reads info 3001 from register 2 with start address 0x0002 in the frame, and parameter 1107 from register 14 (0x000E)."
 },
 "default_word_order": "ABCD",
 "firmware": "Studer system release R706 (appendix V1.6.44); Xcom-485i protocol V1.1.3",
 "source": {
  "title": "Technical specification Studer Modbus RTU Appendix V1.6.44 (with Technical specification Studer Modbus RTU Protocol V1.1.3)",
  "doc_number": "Appendix V1.6.44 (R706); Protocol V1.1.3",
  "revision_date": "2025-09-03",
  "url": "https://studer-innotec.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Manuel-xcom-485i-technical-specification.zip",
  "pages": "Appendix pp. 26-29 (Xcom-CAN BMS parameters pp. 26-28, infos pp. 28-29); Protocol spec pp. 4-8 (bus settings, unit addressing), pp. 18-25 (examples, offsets, encoding). Both PDFs are inside the ZIP."
 },
 "notes": [
  "The Xcom-CAN BMS uses the same unit IDs (60/61) as the BSP; only one of the two can be in an installation.",
  "Every info and parameter is an IEEE 754 single-precision float in two registers, high word first. The protocol spec's example reads Xtender info 3001 as 0x41D3 0x3333 = 26.4 °C, and parameter 1107 as 0x4170 0x0000 = 15.0 A.",
  "Each request must read or write exactly 2 registers (one value); asking for more returns an exception. Infos use function 04, parameters function 03 to read and 16 to write.",
  "Parameter registers have three more views at fixed offsets: +2000 = minimum allowed value, +4000 = maximum allowed value (read only), +6000 = write to RAM only. Use the RAM-only offset for anything written repeatedly; flash is only guaranteed for about 1000 writes per parameter.",
  "Enumerated infos count 0, 1, 2 ...; enumerated parameters use one bit per option (1, 2, 4, 8), still sent as a float. Codes shown as '---' in the appendix are unused and left out of the value lists.",
  "Register names keep the Studer info/parameter number in braces, e.g. {3000}. The register is 2 x (number - base) for that device family.",
  "Parameters whose appendix format is MENU are menu headings, not values, and are left out. The appendix's per-row remarks column is not reproduced.",
  "The gateway's own input registers (pending messages at 0-4, device counts at 5-9, gateway identity at 10-14, on unit 1) and the system clock (holding registers 0-7 on unit 2) are described in the protocol spec but live at other unit IDs, so they are not in this register list.",
  "The protocol spec's first read example prints the value as 0x4170 in the PDU table but 0x41F0 in the serial-line frame; 0x4170 matches the stated 15.0 A."
 ],
